  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 402 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will bring dry conditions and mainly clear\nskies today, although some clouds may linger across the east as\nnorthwest flow aloft continues. North northwest winds will be \nmuch reduced today as well, with more steady winds lingering \nacross the east. Low afternoon RH in the 20s will be found in \nthe west today, although light winds should limit any fire \nweather concerns today. Look for near normal highs today in the \nmid 70s to mid 80s. Tonight, mainly dry conditions look to \ncontinue. There are some CAMs that bring isolated showers to the\nsouthwest along a weak boundary. An overall dry atmosphere and \nlack of instability should limit this threat, thus the outgoing \nforecast remains dry. Winds will become light and variable for \nthe most part tonight. Clear skies could combine with these \nlight winds to produce some patchy fog, although dry air in \nplace will limit this potential so have not included it in the \ncurrent forecast. Lows tonight will be in the mid 40s to mid \n50s. The surface high moves across the eastern half of the state\non Thursday, with an upper level ridge settling in across the \nwest. The result will be another mainly dry day with continued \nnear to slightly above normal high temperatures in the upper 70s\nto upper 80s. Developing surface low to the west will switch \nthe winds to the south, and be sustained at 10 to 20 mph across \nthe west. Meanwhile low afternoon RH values in the 20s could \nalso be found in the west on Thursday. This could bring some \nincreased fire weather concerns to these areas, although winds \nare expected to remain short of critical fire weather criteria. \nSurface low moves more into western MT Thursday night, bringing \na steady southerly wind to much of the area. Meanwhile the ridge\naloft breaks down, lifting an elevated warm front across the \nstate. This could bring some isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms across the west Thursday night. Right now \ninstability and shear look low to modest Thursday night, \nalthough the location of the low level jet may help overcome \nthis. For now the severe weather threat is low yet worth \nmonitoring.\n\nThe initial upper level wave and surface low then looks to move\nthrough Friday. The warm front associated with this feature\ncould bring high temperatures into the 80s to lower 90s for much\nof the area. The potential for severe weather will also return\nwith this feature, although timing of the wave will determine\nwhere thunderstorms set up. If thunderstorms can develop early\nenough across the CWA, large hail and damaging winds will be \nthe main threat. SPC has clipped parts of south central and \nsoutheastern portions of the CWA with a Marginal Risk for severe\nweather on Friday to account for this potential. This area \nlooks to have an additional push of moisture which could help \nwith convection. Lingering low level jet aloft could also \nprovide some increased shear in this area further increasing the\nchances for severe weather, and may also bring a breezy south\nwind to the CWA. The shear vector may also favor discrete \nsupercells, at least initially, along this boundary. An isolated\ntornado would also be possible along the warm front in this \narea, although is very conditional and may be more favored in \nSouth Dakota where the better shear is. Overall this set up \ncould be the next chance for at least isolated severe \nthunderstorms and will be worth monitoring for the latest \nupdates. Zonal flow aloft with embedded shortwaves, combined \nwith a lingering surface low, could continue near daily chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms through the weekend. CSU- MLP \nalso showing the isolated severe weather threat could continue \nthrough the weekend. Warm front stalls on Saturday, lingering \nslightly above normal temperatures in the 80s. A weak cold front\ncould move through Sunday, slightly cooling temperatures yet \nstill bringing near normal highs. Mild lows in the 50s to lower \n60s will continue through the weekend. Fire weather concerns \nFriday through the weekend overall look low. Some low RH values \nwill be found in the west on Friday, although the breezier \nsoutherly winds will be located more in central and eastern \nportions. The rest of the weekend sees improved humidity values \nwith generally light winds. Southwesterly pattern aloft then \nlooks to continue into early next week, bringing more near to \nslightly above normal temperatures along with near daily chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms.",
